 "result_text": "I fixed the MySQL `DOUBLE(11,2)` rendering. Before the change, a MySQL `DOUBLE(11, 2)` column came out as `Double(11, True)`. Now it comes out as `DOUBLE(11, 2)`. I checked this with a mock MySQL engine rather than a real database, and the existing test suite still passes (94 tests). I added no tests, as you asked.\n\nThe cause was in `get_adapted_type` in `src/sqlacodegen/generators.py`. It swapped the reflected MySQL type for the generic `Double`, which has no scale, so the scale was lost. The `2` then ended up in the `asdecimal` slot and rendered as `True`. The compile-equality check didn't catch it, because on this SQLAlchemy version (2.0.55) both types compile to plain `DOUBLE`.\n\nThe fix is a check that skips the substitution when the original type has a scale and the replacement doesn't, so the original MySQL type is kept. Types without a scale, such as plain `DOUBLE`, are still adapted as before.",
